tty: show a laptop's own panel more or less colourful than sRGB

niri sends sRGB to a panel as it is, which on a panel whose primaries
are sRGB's is accurate and, beside a phone or the vivid profile a
laptop's own system ships with, looks washed out. A laptop's panel can
now be shown more colourful, in the display engine: its degamma curve
takes the colours to light, its colour matrix moves each away from the
grey of its own luminance, after any hold to sRGB, and its gamma curve
encodes them again, at no cost to drawing. Greys and white stay as
they were.

NIRI_PANEL_SATURATION in niri's environment sets it as niri starts,
from 0 to 2 with 1 as it is, so a config written for this niri still
parses in one without the patch. The SetPanelSaturation action changes
it while niri runs, for choosing it by eye; it's IPC alone, marked
knuffel(skip), so the config can't name it. Other screens are left as
they are.

